  um and then ultimately you end up with  an entire industry  that uh supports other industries that  are trying to be  trying to deny the science so you've got  the the law firms the pr firms the  advertising agencies  and these so-called non-profits that  that become advocates  uh to oppose regulations so i mean i  i try to write about this partly from a  kind of psychological perspective  looking at the social science and i  think what happens is that  for the the individuals in the  corporations that confront  these charges that there is a very  natural  instinctive inclination to deny it which  is  just part of human nature i think it  then moves pretty quickly into a  corporate strategy  and then it moves from there into its  own  kind of a denial industry with the same  pr firms and others appearing again and  again  and then i think it moves into an  ideology and and so it's that  progression  that makes denial uh this very human  thing  but but promoted by these very powerful  institutions  such a destructive thing do you have any  insights  into the psychology of the in the cogs  in the wheel so to speak  that are carrying out a lot of these  these denial campaigns because i could  imagine a situation where  the people working at the pr firm or the  people working at a corporation or the  law firm  at the individual level many of them  probably know that what they're doing is  lying and that they're denying  what what what are the facts is it as  simple as listen i'm a cog in the wheel  i'm just trying to put food on the table  and this is going to happen  regardless or have you had any insights  into that at the individual level  i think that is definitely part of it um  and corporations themselves especially  the big ones  are designed to make the individual not  feel responsible for what the  corporation does  it does that through division of labor  you're just a cog in a wheel and and we  know for sure that diminishes your in  your  feelings of responsibility for the  consequences  you've got management divided from  ownership  you've got this ideology that tells  managers that their only responsibility  is to maximize shareholder pressure  shareholder profit meanwhile  shareholders don't feel responsible  because they're far away it's  transactional they may just own the  stock for a little while and  they don't know what's going on anyway  we also know that power  tends to focus the mind on your most  salient goals which if you're in a  corporate executive is profit  it also there's some evidence seems to  reduce your  risk awareness and your risk perception  once you become aware of it and your  general  social responsibility again there's the  tribalism the competitiveness inherent  in  incorporations the market mindset so  yeah there's there are a lot of  psychological things you could point to  in fact i  suggest in the book that if you are a  super villain trying to get the world to  destroy itself  by taking crazy risks um you might just  create a society very much like our  corporate dominated market society  because there are many many things there  that undermine our innate sense  of social responsibility very well said  the book is  industrial strength denial eight stories  of corporations defending the  indefensible from the slave trade to  climate change we've been speaking with  the book's author barbara freeze  barbara thank you so much for your time  today thank you dave i appreciate it  the david pakman show at davidpakman.com  [Music]  the david pakman show depends on the  support of our viewers and listeners and  one way to keep us going is through  patreon  where you can pledge as little as one  dollar per month  or as much as you want at patreon.com  slash david pakman show great perks and  benefits as well read about them  the david pakman show at davidpakman.com  dr anthony fauci is at least partially  blaming fox news host tucker carlson's  comments about him for the threats that  he and 